Stabwound wrote:
...
> There is also the issue of the lower achivement trophies (with bells) being 
> worth so many points compared to the much harder achievement trophies. 
> Example: Double Top with bells (3 ascensions in a row) is worth +8 points, 
> while The Full Monty (ascending every role, race, conduct) is worth only +7 
> points.

OK, I think I may have a solution to this finally: we tie the Bells 
bonus to the variety of the attributes being tracked.

So, consider a base bonus "n":

1)	The Birdie tracks 2 potential values (gender: 2): 2n

2)	The Double Top tracks 5 potential values (gender: 2, alignment: 3): 5n

3)	The Hat Trick tracks 10 potential values (gender: 2, alignment: 3, 
race: 5): 10n

4)	The Grand Slam looks at 23 values (gender: 2, alignment: 3, race: 5, 
role: 13): 23n

5)	The Full Monty looks at 35 values (gender: 2, alignment: 3, race: 5, 
role: 13, conduct: 12): 35n

We currently treat this as a multiplier, but that's probably not a good 
idea in this scheme since a Full Monty with Bells On would be worth 7 X 
35 points, which would completely swamp any other clan points.  ;-)

Treating it as an additive bonus would work, though.  And that would 
reduce the question to : what is "n"?

1 is an obvious candidate, making the Full Monty with Bells On worth 35 
points which may be way too much.

1/2 is also an obvious candidate, nicely making the lowest bonus 1 and 
setting highest to 17.5 (a tad higher than the current x2).

I'd suggest changing to this additive system like this, with the base 
value set to 1/2.

> Also, no offense, but why is the Unknown Challenge worth 2 points (more than 
> an ascension?) Looking at the number of people that have completed it 
> already (and a majority of them having not even ascended) I think it should 
> be worth less.
...

Dunno, really.  ;-)

I'd be up for dropping it to 1, and lowering the known challenges to 1/2.


